WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:07.080
A glance. A voice. A change of expression.

2
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:13.480
In every conversation, the brain weaves these signals together in a fraction of a second.

3
00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:17.410
Can an embodied machine learn to do the same?

4
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.180
At the SEIL Lab, University of Surrey,

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.690
we connect human social cognition with embodied artificial intelligence.

6
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.660
It begins with people.

7
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:35.040
With behavioural experiments, eye tracking, EEG and neuroimaging,

8
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:42.170
we study how gaze, faces, gestures and voices are integrated, and when they compete.

9
00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:47.650
Here, a participant locates a voice while an avatar shifts its gaze.

10
00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:53.740
When the two disagree, performance drops: a measurable trace of crossmodal conflict.

11
00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:59.530
We also map how the brain responds to unfairness, whether it affects us or others.

12
00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:03.410
These mechanisms become computational models.

13
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.950
Combining human eye movements with binaural sound localisation,

14
00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:11.750
our deep learning models predict where attention will go.

15
00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:18.420
Scanpath models turn sound and video into gaze shifts a robot can actually perform.

16
00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.650
Then, we give the models a body.

17
00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.740
Facing the same task as our participants,

18
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:30.700
the humanoid robot iCub showed a human-like pattern of crossmodal conflict,

19
00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.080
though people still performed better.

20
00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:38.070
With HRI-Free, iCub watches recorded social scenes,

21
00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:42.380
and its gaze is scored against human attention maps in simulation,

22
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.930
making evaluation scalable and reproducible.

23
00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.490
Finally, robots meet people.

24
00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:55.630
In a shape-sorting game, two people worked together while iCub gave instructions.

25
00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:02.840
Its happy face helped teams finish faster, and its gaze changed how intelligent it seemed.

26
00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:06.940
When robots mirror our head movements and expressions,

27
00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:13.120
the way they sense and express changes how responsive and human-like they seem.

28
00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:16.700
Each step feeds the next:

29
00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.650
human evidence constrains models, robots make them testable,

30
00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.910
and people's responses raise new questions about the mind.

31
00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:34.160
Led by Dr Di Fu, across the School of Psychology and the Surrey Institute for People-Centred AI,

32
00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:40.380
our team brings together psychology, neuroscience, computer science and robotics.

33
00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:45.110
Seil means rope in German.

34
00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:48.050
In English, it sounds like sail.
